#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074958 :tutorial/content "Fraction multiplication occurs regularly in real-life situations.\n\nFor example, suppose that a bag contains\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\\,\\text{kg}\n$$\n\nof flour. If Nancy used\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nof the bag to make a pie, how much flour did she use to make the pie?\n\nTo find out how much flour Nancy used, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nby\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo multiply these two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\times \\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{3\\times 2}{5\\times 5} = \\dfrac{6}{25}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Nancy used\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{25}\n$$\n\nkilograms of flour." :tutorial/id #uuid "a638bc3c-9858-5cb6-a95b-ba0a356c4d1b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1894 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
